To address this issue, we present OpenFloodPlan, a method intended to provide an operational flood-mitigation planning pipeline based entirely on open-source data and software. Key contributions include:
1) A reproducible data ingestion and preprocessing chain for SRTM 30 m DEM, Sentinel-1 SAR, Copernicus Land Cover, OpenStreetMap, NASA GPM rainfall and in-situ gauges;
2) A hybrid hydrologic and hydraulic modeling procedure combining a calibrated Rational/TR-55 style approach and 1D/2D HEC-RAS coupling for urban channels;
3) A multi-objective mitigation optimization that balances cost, reduction in flooded area, and social vulnerability with explicit budget constraints;
4) A deployed proof-of-concept on the Ciliwung sub-basin that reports quantitative performance metrics including mapping accuracy (95.4%) and intervention effectiveness (38% median area reduction).
